  "statusText": "Calm, risk-on conditions persist into the late-Asia/early-Europe handover; Applied Materials' after-hours earnings reaction stays contained to the single name, with Asian chip-equipment and memory peers trading higher rather than following it down, though this run had zero Alpha Vantage access (daily quota already exhausted) and relies entirely on WebSearch.",
  "recommendation": "Current action remains EA_ON. Reassess through the European morning for any delayed semiconductor-equipment read-through, and again ahead of today's 12:30 UTC Retail Sales, 13:30 UTC cash open and 14:00 UTC preliminary UMich sentiment cluster; an upgrade to a higher Tail state requires observed persistent one-way structure with failed pullbacks in the broad Nasdaq-100 complex itself, not a single-name earnings whipsaw, the pending data cluster, or this run's Alpha Vantage outage.",
  "headline": "Nasdaq-100 stays calm into the European handover after Thursday's fresh S&P 500 record above 7,800 -- VIX ~14.7 (near a 7-month low), 10Y ~4.65% -- as Applied Materials' confirmed ~4-5% after-hours slide stays a contained single-name reaction while Asian chip-equipment peers Tokyo Electron and Advantest trade higher; Alpha Vantage was fully rate-limited this run (daily quota exhausted), so this assessment relies entirely on WebSearch.",
  "body": "Roughly an hour past the prior run and about 5.5 hours after Thursday's 20:00 UTC cash close, conditions remain calm and essentially unchanged into the late-Asia/early-Europe handover. Thursday's session closed with the S&P 500 at a fresh record 7,798.99 (+0.65%, intraday high 7,816.70) and the Nasdaq Composite up 0.81% to 26,803.03, on a soft July PPI print reinforcing a September Fed-hold narrative; oil fell into the close. The VIX sits near a seven-month low (~14.68) and the 10-year Treasury yield holds around 4.65%, both essentially flat from the prior run. WTI trades near $82.7 and Brent near $87.1, little changed. Applied Materials beat on EPS ($3.50 vs $3.45 est.) but missed narrowly on revenue ($9.115B vs $9.176B est.), guided 30%+ 2026 semiconductor-equipment growth, and fell roughly 3.9-5.2% after hours (sources vary slightly) against an options market that had priced an expected ~14% ($75) move -- i.e. the actual reaction landed well inside the pre-earnings expected range. Critically, this run found direct evidence against broad negative transmission: in Friday's Asian session, chip-equipment and memory peers Tokyo Electron (+2.13%), Advantest (+4.02%), SK Hynix (+6%) and Samsung (+2.05%) all traded higher, tracking broad US tech strength, lower oil and the soft PPI read, while S&P 500 and Nasdaq-100 futures held just above the flatline. This is a materially firmer read than the prior run's inconclusive check and supports treating the AMAT move as a contained single-name earnings reaction rather than a sector-wide risk event. Geopolitically, the Strait of Hormuz standoff continues unresolved (Iran's parliament reviewing a bill to bar US/Israel-linked vessels, which the White House has rejected) with no new kinetic escalation identified since the prior run. Alpha Vantage's free-tier daily quota (25 requests/day) was already exhausted at the start of this run -- the first TREASURY_YIELD call returned an immediate rate_limit error -- so, per the fallback protocol, this assessment relies entirely on WebSearch for yields, oil, VIX, equities and price-path context; Confidence is held in the medium-low band to reflect that reduced evidence base and the absence of a directly-confirmed live NQ/QQQ intraday print. Tail holds low at 11% (calm continuation, no persistent one-way structure, and now clearer cross-asset non-confirmation of the AMAT reaction) and Stress eases slightly to 27% as the isolated-reaction read firms up, though the approaching Friday US data cluster (Retail Sales, cash open, preliminary UMich sentiment, the latter expected to soften to 54.1 from 55.2) keeps Stress from falling further. Confidence is 45% (medium-low), reflecting the full Alpha Vantage outage and reliance on secondary web reporting, partially offset by strong multi-source corroboration on the AMAT/Asia read-through.",
